loan_payment <- function(P, r, n) {
  P * r / (1 - (1 + r)^(-n))
}
function() creates a function object, which is assigned to the name loan_payment with <-. P, r and n are the arguments supplied by the user. The body inside the braces computes the annuity formula; R returns the value of the last expression evaluated (you could also write return(...)). Calling loan_payment(10000, 0.005, 24) gives about 443.21, so the monthly payment is roughly £443.21. Using named arguments, loan_payment(P = 10000, r = 0.005, n = 24), makes the call easier to read.

years_to_target <- function(start, rate, target) {
  if (rate <= 0) stop("rate must be positive")
  balance <- start
  years <- 0
  while (balance < target) {
    balance <- balance * (1 + rate)
    years <- years + 1
  }
  years
}
years_to_target(1000, 0.07, 2000) returns 11: after 10 years the balance is 1000 × 1.07¹⁰ ≈ £1,967, still short of £2,000, and after 11 years it is about £2,105. The check on rate matters because with a zero or negative rate the balance never reaches a higher target and the loop would run forever. (If target is already below start the function correctly returns 0.)
